    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Early societies urged moderate drinking, which is why the movement bears the name temperance; from the 1830s teetotalism demanded complete abstinence, and later reformers sought laws against the sale of alcohol.</li><li>By 1900, scholars have estimated, one in ten Americans had signed a pledge to abstain, and the movement became the most well-organised lobby group of its time.</li><li>National prohibitions were introduced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> between 1918 and 1920 and were repealed, the American one in 1933, after critics pointed to bootlegging, crime and lost tax revenue.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1767 the average <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> family was consuming seven barrels of hard cider a year, about 35 gallons per person, in the colonies where the temperance movement later took shape.</p></aside>
    </section>

